Since you didn't provide a specific UVL (Unified Vocabulary Language) schema or a specific domain (as UVL is often used in specialized industrial or academic modeling), I have translated your text into a **standardized, object-oriented UVL structure**.

In UVL, we define the entities (classes) and their relationships (associations).

```uvl
// Definition of the System Entities
Class Computer {
    // A computer can have multiple software applications installed
    Association installedSoftware : Software [0..*];
}

Class Software {
    Attribute name : String;
    Attribute version : String;
}

// Specialization of Software for Text Editing
Class TextEditingProgram extends Software {
    Attribute editingCapability : String;
    // Examples: MS Word, Pages
}

// Specialization of Software for Cell Calculation
Class CellCalculationTool extends Software {
    Attribute calculationCapability : String;
    // Examples: MS Excel, Numbers
}

// Instance Mapping (The specific scenario described)
Instance myComputer : Computer {
    installedSoftware = {
        Instance wordProcessor : TextEditingProgram {
            name = "MS Word / Pages";
        },
        Instance spreadsheetTool : CellCalculationTool {
            name = "MS Excel / Numbers";
        }
    };
}
```

### Syntax Explanations:
1.  **`Class`**: Used to define the general categories (Computer, Software).
2.  **`extends`**: Used for inheritance. Since both a text editor and a spreadsheet tool are types of software, they inherit from the `Software` class.
3.  **`Association`**: Defines the relationship. The `[0..*]` syntax indicates that a computer can have zero to many software programs installed.
4.  **`Instance`**: This translates the specific "A computer has..." statement into a concrete object mapping.
5.  **`Attribute`**: Used to store the specific names mentioned in your text (MS Word, Excel, etc.).<turn|>